W:Near Banbridge Town in the County Down
W:One morning last July
W:A boreen green came a sweet colleen
W:And she smiled as she passed me by
W:
W:She looked so sweet from her two bare feet
W:To the sheen of her nut brown hair
W:Such a coaxing elf, sure I shook myself
W:For to see I was really there
W:
W:From Bantry Bay down to Derry Quay
W:And from Galway to Dublin Town
W:No maid I've seen like the brown colleen
W:That I met in the County Down
W:
W:As she onward sped, sure I scratched my head
W:And I looked with a feelin' rare
W:And I says, says I, to a passer-by
W:"Whose the maid with the nut brown hair?"
W:
W:He smiled at me and he says's, says's he
W:"That's the gem of Ireland's crown
W:Young Rosie McCann from the banks of a Bann
W:She's the star of the County Down"
W:
W:From Bantry Bay down to Derry Quay
W:And from Galway to Dublin Town
W:No maid I've seen like the brown colleen
W:That I met in the County Down, yeah
W:
W:At the Harvest Fair, she'll be surely there
W:I'll dress in my Sunday clothes
W:My shoes shone bright and my hat cocked
W:Right for a smile from my nut brown rose
W:
W:No pipe I'll smoke, no horse I'll yoke
W:'Til my plough turns rust colored brown
W:'Til a smiling bride, by my own fireside
W:Sits the star of the County Down
W:
W:From Bantry Bay down to Derry Quay
W:And from Galway to Dublin Town
W:No maid I've seen like the brown colleen
W:That I met in the County Down
